IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `wa-interpretation-nouns`

-- Interpretation Act 1984 (Western Australia) -- PART IX: PROCEDURES AND PENALTIES (ss 66 to 72).
-- Sections 68 and 70 are deleted.  These sections say what rules of court are, which of the two kinds of offence a
-- charge is, whether penal laws reach a company, how a continuing failure is punished, and where a penalty is stated.

§ `Part IX -- Procedures and penalties`

§§ `66. Rules of court`

-- Section 66(1): rules of court, for a court or tribunal, are rules made by the authority that has for the time being
-- power to make rules or orders regulating its practice and procedure.
-- src:657 | (1) In a written law, rules of court, in relation to any court or tribunal, means rules made by the authority having for the time being power to make rules or orders regulating the practice and procedure of such court or tribunal.
@ref s 66(1) (src:657)
GIVEN `they were made by the authority having for the time being power to make rules or orders regulating the practice and procedure of the court or tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`they are rules of court` `they were made by the authority having for the time being power to make rules or orders regulating the practice and procedure of the court or tribunal` MEANS
    `they were made by the authority having for the time being power to make rules or orders regulating the practice and procedure of the court or tribunal`

-- Section 66(2): the authority's power includes power to make rules of court for the purpose of any written law that
-- directs or authorises anything to be done by or in accordance with rules of court.
-- src:658 | (2) The power of an authority referred to in subsection (1) includes a power to make rules of court for the purpose of any written law which directs or authorises anything to be done by or in accordance with rules of court.
@ref s 66(2) (src:658)
GIVEN `a written law directs or authorises something to be done by or in accordance with rules of court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the authority's power includes making rules of court for the purpose of the written law` `a written law directs or authorises something to be done by or in accordance with rules of court` MEANS
    IF `a written law directs or authorises something to be done by or in accordance with rules of court` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `Way a written law deals with the jurisdiction of a court or tribunal` IS ONE OF
    `it confers jurisdiction`
    `it extends jurisdiction`
    `it varies jurisdiction`
    `it does none of these`

-- Section 66(3): where a written law confers, extends or varies the jurisdiction of a court or tribunal, the authority
-- with power to regulate its practice and procedure may make rules or orders, including about costs, for regulating the
-- practice and procedure in the exercise of the jurisdiction so conferred, extended or varied.
-- src:659 | (3) Where a written law confers any jurisdiction on a court or other tribunal or extends or varies the jurisdiction of a court or other tribunal, the authority having for the time being power to make rules or orders regulating the practice and procedure of that court or tribunal may make rules or orders, including rules or orders with respect to costs, for the purpose of regulating the practice and procedure of that court or tribunal in the exercise of the jurisdiction so conferred, extended or varied.
@ref s 66(3) (src:659)
GIVEN x IS A `Way a written law deals with the jurisdiction of a court or tribunal`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the authority may make rules or orders, including about costs, for the practice and procedure in exercising the jurisdiction` x MEANS
    CONSIDER x
    WHEN `it does none of these` THEN NOTHING
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `67. Offences and proceedings for offences`

DECLARE `Kind of offence` IS ONE OF
    `an indictable offence`
    `a simple offence`

DECLARE `How an offence is designated` IS ONE OF
    `designated as a crime`
    `designated as a misdemeanour`
    `designated as an indictable offence`
    `designated as a simple offence`
    `not otherwise designated`

-- Section 67(1), (1a) and (2): offences are of two kinds, indictable and simple.  One designated a crime or a
-- misdemeanour is indictable; one not otherwise designated is simple.
-- src:661 | (1) Offences are of 2 kinds: indictable offences and simple offences.
-- src:662 | (1a) An offence designated as a crime or as a misdemeanour is an indictable offence.
-- src:663 | (2) An offence not otherwise designated is a simple offence.
@ref s 67(1) (src:661)
@ref s 67(1a) (src:662)
@ref s 67(2) (src:663)
GIVEN x IS A `How an offence is designated`
GIVETH A `Kind of offence`
`the kind of offence it is` x MEANS
    CONSIDER x
    WHEN `designated as a crime` THEN `an indictable offence`
    WHEN `designated as a misdemeanour` THEN `an indictable offence`
    WHEN `designated as an indictable offence` THEN `an indictable offence`
    WHEN `designated as a simple offence` THEN `a simple offence`
    WHEN `not otherwise designated` THEN `a simple offence`

-- Section 67(3): the procedure for prosecuting and dealing with offences is set out in the Criminal Procedure Act 2004.
-- src:664 | (3) The procedure for prosecuting and dealing with offences is set out in the Criminal Procedure Act 2004.
@ref s 67(3) (src:664)
GIVETH A STRING
`the Act that sets out the procedure for prosecuting and dealing with offences` MEANS "Criminal Procedure Act 2004"

-- Section 67(5): the section does not limit the operation of Part 3 of the Children's Court of Western Australia Act 1988.
-- src:666 | (5) This section does not limit the operation of Part 3 of the Children’s Court of Western Australia Act 1988.
@ref s 67(5) (src:666)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 67 limits the operation of Part 3 of the Children's Court of Western Australia Act 1988` MEANS FALSE

§§ `69. Bodies corporate, application of penal laws to`

-- Section 69(1): every enactment relating to an offence punishable on indictment or on summary conviction refers to
-- bodies corporate as well as to individuals.
-- src:670 | (1) Every enactment relating to an offence punishable on indictment or on summary conviction shall be taken to refer to bodies corporate as well as to individuals.
@ref s 69(1) (src:670)
GIVEN `the enactment relates to an offence punishable on indictment or on summary conviction` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the enactment refers to the person` `the enactment relates to an offence punishable on indictment or on summary conviction` `the person is a body corporate` MEANS
    IF `the enactment relates to an offence punishable on indictment or on summary conviction`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- Section 69(2): where a penalty or sum of money is payable to a party aggrieved, it is payable to a body corporate in
-- every case where that body is the party aggrieved.
-- src:671 | (2) Where under a written law, a penalty or sum of money is payable to a party aggrieved, it shall be payable to a body corporate in every case where that body is the party aggrieved.
@ref s 69(2) (src:671)
GIVEN `a penalty or sum of money is payable to a party aggrieved` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the party aggrieved is a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the penalty or sum is payable to the body corporate` `a penalty or sum of money is payable to a party aggrieved` `the party aggrieved is a body corporate` MEANS
    IF `a penalty or sum of money is payable to a party aggrieved` AND `the party aggrieved is a body corporate`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `71. Continuing offences`

-- Section 71(1)(a) to (c): a written law requires or directs an act or thing to be done within a particular period or
-- before a particular time, failing to do it then is an offence, and it was not done then.  The consequences in (d) to
-- (f) follow only then.
-- src:675 | (1) Where —
-- src:676 | (a) by or under a written law an act or thing is required or directed to be done within a particular period or before a particular time; and
-- src:677 | (b) failure to do that act or thing within the period or before the time referred to in paragraph (a) constitutes an offence; and
-- src:678 | (c) that act or thing is not done within the period or before the time referred to in paragraph (a),
-- src:679 | the following provisions have effect —
@ref s 71(1) (src:675-679)
GIVEN `the act or thing is required or directed to be done within a particular period or before a particular time` IS A BOOLEAN
      `failure to do it within the period or before the time is an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is not done within the period or before the time` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the provisions of section 71(1)(d) to (f) have effect` `the act or thing is required or directed to be done within a particular period or before a particular time` `failure to do it within the period or before the time is an offence` `it is not done within the period or before the time` MEANS
        `the act or thing is required or directed to be done within a particular period or before a particular time`
    AND `failure to do it within the period or before the time is an offence`
    AND `it is not done within the period or before the time`

-- Section 71(1)(d): the obligation continues, although the period has expired or the time has passed, until the act
-- or thing is done.
-- src:680 | (d) the obligation to do that act or thing continues, notwithstanding that that period has expired or that time has passed, until that act or thing is done; and
@ref s 71(1)(d) (src:680)
GIVEN `section 71(1) applies` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the act or thing has been done` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the obligation to do the act or thing continues` `section 71(1) applies` `the act or thing has been done` MEANS
    `section 71(1) applies` AND (NOT `the act or thing has been done`)

-- Section 71(1)(e) and 71(2), the count: a person convicted is guilty of a separate and further offence for each day AFTER
-- the day of the conviction during which the failure continues.  The day of the conviction is not counted.
-- src:681 | (e) where a person is convicted of an offence that, by virtue of paragraph (d), is constituted by failure to do that act or thing after the expiration of that period or after that time, as the case may be, that person is guilty of a separate and further offence in respect of each day after the day of the conviction during which the failure to do that act or thing continues; and
-- src:687 | that person is guilty of a separate and further offence in respect of each day after the day of the conviction during which the failure to do that act or thing continues and, unless otherwise provided, the penalty applicable to each such separate and further offence is $50.
@ref s 71(1)(e) (src:681)
@ref s 71(2) (src:687)
GIVEN `the day of the conviction` IS A DATE
      `the last day on which the failure to do the act or thing continues` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of separate and further offences` `the day of the conviction` `the last day on which the failure to do the act or thing continues` MEANS
    IF Day `the last day on which the failure to do the act or thing continues` GREATER THAN Day `the day of the conviction`
    THEN Day `the last day on which the failure to do the act or thing continues` MINUS Day `the day of the conviction`
    ELSE 0

-- Section 71(1)(f) and 71(2): unless otherwise provided, the penalty for each separate and further offence is $50.
-- src:682 | (f) unless otherwise provided, the penalty applicable to each separate and further offence is $50.
@ref s 71(1)(f) (src:682)
GIVEN `the penalty the written law provides for each further offence, if it provides one` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the penalty for each separate and further offence` `the penalty the written law provides for each further offence, if it provides one` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the penalty the written law provides for each further offence, if it provides one`
    WHEN JUST provided THEN provided
    WHEN NOTHING THEN 50

-- Section 71(2): the case where no period or time is specified.  The offence of failing to do what is required, and a
-- conviction for it, are what bring the daily offences in.
-- src:683 | (2) Where —
-- src:684 | (a) by or under a written law an act or thing is required or directed to be done but no period within which or time by which that act or thing is to be done is specified; and
-- src:685 | (b) failure to do that act or thing constitutes an offence; and
-- src:686 | (c) a person is convicted of an offence in respect of a failure to do that act or thing,
-- src:687 | that person is guilty of a separate and further offence in respect of each day after the day of the conviction during which the failure to do that act or thing continues and, unless otherwise provided, the penalty applicable to each such separate and further offence is $50.
@ref s 71(2) (src:683-687)
GIVEN `the act or thing is required or directed to be done but no period or time is specified` IS A BOOLEAN
      `failure to do it is an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a person is convicted of an offence in respect of the failure` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the daily offences of section 71(2) arise` `the act or thing is required or directed to be done but no period or time is specified` `failure to do it is an offence` `a person is convicted of an offence in respect of the failure` MEANS
        `the act or thing is required or directed to be done but no period or time is specified`
    AND `failure to do it is an offence`
    AND `a person is convicted of an offence in respect of the failure`

-- Section 71(1)(e): a person convicted of the offence that paragraph (d) makes is guilty of the further offences.
-- src:681 | (e) where a person is convicted of an offence that, by virtue of paragraph (d), is constituted by failure to do that act or thing after the expiration of that period or after that time, as the case may be, that person is guilty of a separate and further offence in respect of each day after the day of the conviction during which the failure to do that act or thing continues; and
@ref s 71(1)(e) (src:681)
GIVEN `section 71(1) applies` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a person is convicted of an offence that, by virtue of paragraph (d), is constituted by failure to do the act or thing after the period or time` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the person is guilty of separate and further offences` `section 71(1) applies` `a person is convicted of an offence that, by virtue of paragraph (d), is constituted by failure to do the act or thing after the period or time` MEANS
        `section 71(1) applies`
    AND `a person is convicted of an offence that, by virtue of paragraph (d), is constituted by failure to do the act or thing after the period or time`

§§ `72. Statutory penalties, construction of`

DECLARE `Where a penalty is specified in an Act` IS ONE OF
    `at the foot of a section, without qualification`
    `at the foot of a subsection, but not at the foot of the section`
    `at the foot of a section and expressed to apply to specified subsections`
    `somewhere else`

DECLARE `What is an offence under section 72` IS ONE OF
    `a contravention of the section`
    `a contravention of the subsection`
    `a contravention of any of the specified subsections`

-- Section 72(1): where an Act specifies a penalty in one of the three ways (a) to (c), then, unless the contrary is
-- expressly provided, a contravention of the section or subsection, or of any of the subsections, is an offence, the
-- penalty on conviction for which is the penalty specified.  For any other layout the section says nothing.
-- src:689 | (1) Where in an Act a penalty —
-- src:690 | (a) is specified without qualification at the foot of a section of the Act; or
-- src:691 | (b) is specified at the foot of a subsection of a section of the Act, but not at the foot of the section; or
-- src:692 | (c) is specified at the foot of a section of the Act and expressed to apply to a specified subsection or specified subsections of the section,
-- src:693 | then, unless the contrary is expressly provided, a contravention of the section or subsection, or, as the case may be, of any of the subsections, is an offence the penalty on conviction for which is the penalty specified.
@ref s 72(1) (src:689-693)
GIVEN where IS A `Where a penalty is specified in an Act`
      `the contrary is expressly provided` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE `What is an offence under section 72`
`what is an offence with the penalty specified` where `the contrary is expressly provided` MEANS
    IF `the contrary is expressly provided`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE
        CONSIDER where
        WHEN `at the foot of a section, without qualification` THEN JUST `a contravention of the section`
        WHEN `at the foot of a subsection, but not at the foot of the section` THEN JUST `a contravention of the subsection`
        WHEN `at the foot of a section and expressed to apply to specified subsections` THEN JUST `a contravention of any of the specified subsections`
        WHEN `somewhere else` THEN NOTHING

-- Section 72(2): where a penalty is specified as in (1) in respect of a section or subsection that expressly creates an
-- offence, the penalty on conviction is the penalty specified.
-- src:694 | (2) Where in an Act a penalty is specified as described in subsection (1) in respect of a section or subsection that expressly creates an offence, the penalty for that offence on conviction is the penalty specified.
@ref s 72(2) (src:694)
GIVEN where IS A `Where a penalty is specified in an Act`
      `the section or subsection expressly creates an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the penalty specified` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the penalty on conviction` where `the section or subsection expressly creates an offence` `the penalty specified` MEANS
    IF `the section or subsection expressly creates an offence`
    THEN
        CONSIDER where
        WHEN `somewhere else` THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE JUST `the penalty specified`
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `Thing a section or subsection reference might name in a schedule` IS ONE OF
    `a clause of a schedule to an Act`
    `a subclause of a schedule to an Act`
    `something else`

-- Section 72(3): in section 72 a reference to a section or subsection of an Act includes a clause or subclause of a
-- schedule to an Act.
-- src:695 | (3) In this section a reference to a section or subsection of an Act includes a reference to a clause or subclause of a schedule to an Act.
@ref s 72(3) (src:695)
GIVEN x IS A `Thing a section or subsection reference might name in a schedule`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`a reference to a section or subsection of an Act in section 72 includes` x MEANS
    CONSIDER x
    WHEN `something else` THEN NOTHING
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- Section 72(4): the section applies, with necessary modifications, to subsidiary legislation by which offences may be
-- created or penalties may be imposed.  It applies in an Act as it stands.
-- src:696 | (4) This section applies, with any modifications that are necessary, to subsidiary legislation by which offences may be created or penalties may be imposed.
@ref s 72(4) (src:696)
GIVEN kind IS A `Kind of written law`
      `offences may be created or penalties may be imposed by it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`section 72 applies to it, with any modifications that are necessary` kind `offences may be created or penalties may be imposed by it` MEANS
    CONSIDER kind
    WHEN `an Act` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `subsidiary legislation` THEN
        IF `offences may be created or penalties may be imposed by it` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING
